===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{term-label|en|transitive}}

# To [[make#Verb|make]] (something) [[likely]] to be [[easily]] [[damage#Verb|damaged]].
#* {{RQ:NYT|date=21 February 1926|title=Feminist furor: ''Three Kingdoms''. By {{w|Storm Jameson}}. 365 pp. New York: Alfred A. Knopf. [book review]|section=book review|page=8|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_new-york-times_1926-02-21_75_24865/page/n47/mode/1up|column=5|passage=Its method may be roughly said to be the invention, at all events for the main characters in their novels, of a psychology so tortuous and devious that its fantastic contours cannot be fitted into any single act or situation of or in life without an elaborate apparatus of dissertation. The artistic disadvantages of the method are many. One, and perhaps the chief, is a weakening—a "'''flimsying'''" of the structure because a proper proportion of the obvious, which is the thews and sinews of fiction, has, perforce, to be left out.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1981|author=James Nathan Miller|chapter=Buying and Selling at the Pentagon|editors=w:Marc Holzer; Ellen Doree Rosen|title=Current Cases in Public Administration|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper & Row]]|section=section IV (Human Behavior)|page=184|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/currentcasesinpu0000unse/page/184/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-06-042879-2|passage=[W]hen he {{quote-gloss|[[w:A. Ernest Fitzgerald|A[rthur] Ernest Fitzgerald]]}} tried to check reports that Lockheed was seriously "'''flimsying'''" the plane's construction, he received an angry call from Pentagon brass warning him, in effect, to keep out of engineering matters.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|chapter=Facing the Challenges of Time and Place [5.57: On Coming Home from Kampuchea]|title=This We Can Say: Australian Quaker Life, Faith and Thought|location=Kenmore, Qld.|publisher=Faith and Practice Committee, {{w|Australia Yearly Meeting}} of the [[w:Quakers|Religious Society of Friends (Quakers)]]|year_published=2004|page=280|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=P5tHxiLH5lgC&pg=PA280|isbn=978-0-9751579-0-9|passage=Cry for the travesties of homes that now squat meagre on wide concrete bases, / '''flimsied''' in palm-leaf. They will not last, but they will have to do in the thin hungry scrabble to survive.}}
# {{lb|en|dated|or|historical}} To [[type#Verb|type]] or [[write#Verb|write]] ([[text#Noun|text]]) on a [[flimsy#Noun|flimsy]] (“[[sheet#Noun|sheet]] of [[thin#Adjective|thin]] [[typing#Noun|typing]] [[paper#Noun|paper]] [[use#Verb|used]] together with [[carbon paper]] in a [[typewriter]] to [[make#Verb|make]] [[multiple#Adjective|multiple]] [[copy#Noun|copies]] of a [[document#Noun|document]]”) ''(noun {{senseno|en|paper}})''.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1895|month=November|author=Frank Banfield|title=Interviewing in Practice|journal=[[w:National Review (London)|The National Review]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:Edward Arnold (publisher)|Edward Arnold]],{{nb...|37, Bedford Street, Strand, W.C.}}|volume=XXVI|issue=153|page=368|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=QSI_AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA368|oclc=970881143|passage=An interview is not a speech. {{...}} If a man wants to publish an allocution of this kind, he should write it out and give it to me, or anyone else—a newsagency for example—and it will be "'''flimsied'''" to most of our English daily papers, whose conductors would, of course, use their own discretion as to how much or how little of it they would use. But in no sense of the word could such a performance be properly classified under the heading of the interview.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=16 June 1904|author=[[w:Robert Best (politician)|[Robert Wallace] Best]], chairman and questioner; William Chessell, witness|title=Select Committee. Privilege: Case of Senator Lt.-Col. [[w:John Neild|{{quote-gloss|John Cash}} Neild]]. Minutes of Evidence.|journal=Journals of the [[w:Australian Senate|Senate]] and Printed Papers having Special Reference to the Senate|location=[Canberra, A.C.T]|publisher=[F]or the [[w:Australian Government|Government of the Commonwealth of Australia]] by Rob[er]t S. Brain, Government Printer for the State of Victoria|year_published=1904|volume=I|section=paragraphs 908–910|page=79|pageurl=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=uc1.c2630362&seq=665|oclc=73066967|passage=Did you, as a matter of fact, receive pages 55 and 56 at the same time?—I cannot say that I did. / But if you had received them?—I should have '''flimsied''' them with this. / As they are not '''flimsied''', what do you say?—All I can presume is that they were not there. I should not have separated one paper from the other, and '''flimsied''' one and left the other.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=22 June 1907|title=Notes of the week|journal=[[w:Saturday Review (London newspaper)|The Saturday Review of Politics, Literature, Science, and Art]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for the proprietors by Spottiswoode & Co. [...]; and published by}} Reginald Webster Page,{{nb...}}|volume=103|issue=2,695|page=765|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_saturday-review-uk_the-saturday-review_1907-06-22_103_2695/mode/1up|column=2|oclc=1036708132|passage={{...}} Mr. [[w:Spencer Leigh Hughes|Spencer Hughes]], the Liberal, is accusing the Independent Labour men of being "blacklegs." Some of them, it seems, have been doing the work of two or three journalists in the House, '''flimsying''' and syndicating London Letters and Labour articles and notes in a number of newspapers. Cannot they be peacefully persuaded to attend to their own work, and leave journalism to journalists?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1923|author=w:Henry Lucy|chapter=XIX|title=The Diary of a Journalist: Fresh Extracts|location=London|publisher=[[w:John Murray (publishing house)|John Murray]],{{nb...|Albemarle Street, W.}}|volume=III|page=179|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dli.ministry.01687/page/179/mode/1up|oclc=21668331|passage=I {{quote-gloss|[[w:Phil May (caricaturist)|Phil May]]}} lived down Brixton way and made a precarious living by '''flimsying''' news paragraphs and personally delivering copies at the newspaper offices. Sometimes they were used in full, occasionally they were ruthlessly cut down to a few lines; often they went direct to the waste-paper basket.}}
